 On c3: nothing\n- On b2: PWF0\n- On d2: PWF0\n- On e2: PWF0\n- On f2: NBF1 (Black knight)\n- On g2: PWF1\n- On h2: PWF0\n\nWait — confusion. The notation says:\n"P: c7-c5" etc., so the valid moves are from Black\'s perspective.\n\nAlso, on rank 4: a4 has PWF1 (White pawn), c4 has PWT1 — ah! So **c4 has a White pawn that moved two squares last turn** (hence 2Step=T), so it\'s en passant vulnerable.\n\nThat explains why **P: e5-e4** is a valid move — it would be capturing **en passant** on d4? No — e5 to e4 is just a normal push? Wait — no.\n\nWait: **P: e5-e4** — that\'s moving forward one square. But e4 is occupied by a White pawn? Let\'s see:\n\n- e4: currently empty? Looking at the board:\n  - Rank 4: a4=PWF1, c4=PWT1 → so c4 has a White pawn that just moved two squares (from c2 to c4). \n  - e4: blank → so no piece on e4.\n  - But e5 has PBF1 (my pawn).\n\nSo **e5-e4** is a legal quiet pawn move — advancing my e-pawn.\n\nBut more importantly: **c4 has a White pawn that moved two squares last move (PWT1)**, so my pawn on b7 or d7 could potentially capture en passant — but only if adjacent.\n\nMy pawn on b7 (b7-b5 or b7-b6) — if I play b7-b5, then next move White could capture en passant, but right now, **I** could capture en passant on c4 if I had a pawn on d5 or b5.\n\nWait — do I?\n\n- d7-d5 is a valid move → after that, the pawn would be on d5, adjacent to c4 → but en passant must be immediate.\n\nBut d7-d5 is listed as a valid move — and if I play it, since White just played c2-c4 (assumed), then **d7-d5** would allow en passant capture *by White*, not me.\n\nBut here, **I** could play **b7-b5**, putting my pawn on b5, adjacent to c4 — then **next move** White could capture b5xc4 en passant.\n\nBut right now, **can I capture c4 en passant?** Only if I have a pawn on b5 or d5 already. I don’t. So no immediate en passant for me.\n\nBut notice: **P: c7-c5** is a valid move — if I play that, my pawn goes to c5, which is adjacent to b4 and d4 — but no enemy pawn on those files. So no.\n\nWait — White has a pawn on a4 (PWF1) — that didn\'t move two squares (flag=F), so not en passant eligible.\n\nSo only c4 (PWT1) is en passant target.\n\nTo capture it en passant, I’d need a black pawn on b5 or d5.\n\nCurrently, none. But if I play **b7-b5**, then next move White plays c4xb5 en passant.\n\nBut that might open the c-file for my rook.\n\nAlternatively, **d7-d5** opens central tension.\n\nNow, evaluating promising moves:\n\nEvaluation:\n1. **P: c7-c5** – Strong. Challenges the center, supports d5 push later, prepares for knight development (Nc6). A standard opening move.\n2. **P: d7-d5** – Very strong. Immediate central strike. If White captures exd5, I recapture with queen or knight, opening lines. Could lead to dynamic play.\n3. **P: e5-e4** – Pushes my pawn, attacks d3 and f3, gains space. But e4 may become weak later. Also, no support yet — knight on f6 could support, but e4 might get attacked.\n4. **Q: d8-f6** – Threatens check or capture on f2? Qd8-f6 attacks f2, which is only guarded by king — White has N on f2, but if queen takes, Nxf2, then Kxf2 — but queen can be chased. Risky without backup.\n5. **B: f8-c5** – Develops bishop to active diagonal, eyes f2 and g1. Good developing move.\n6. **N: b8-c6** – Natural development, supports e5 or d4 pushes, connects with queen.\n7. **P: b7-b5** – Flank move, aiming for queenside expansion, but slow in the current position.\n\nBest options seem to be **d7-d5** or **c7-c5** for central control, or **Nc6**.\n\nBut **d7-d5** immediately challenges the center. After d7-d5:\n- If White plays e4xd5, I can recapture with Nf6xd5 or Qd8xd5.\n- If they don’t, I control the center.\n- It also activates my light-squared bishop.\n\nCompare to c7-c5 — similar idea, but less direct.\n\nAlso, **Qd8-f6** is listed — but after Qf6, White can play Ng4 or Ne5, attacking queen.\n\nBetter to develop minor pieces first.\n\n**N: b8-c6** is solid, but d7-d5 gains more space.\n\nAnother idea: **P: e5-e4*