\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|KWF1|    |    |    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, N: b8-d7, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, B: c8-d7, B: c8-e6, B: c8-f5, B: c8-g4, B: c8-h3, P: d5-c4, P: d5-d4, Q: d8-d7, Q: d8-d6, Q: d8-e7, Q: d8-f6, Q: d8-g5, Q: d8-h4, P: e4-d3, P: e4-f3, K: e8-d7, K: e8-e7, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, B: f8-e7, B: f8-d6, B: f8-c5, B: f8-b4, B: f8-a3, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and it's my turn to move. White has advanced several pawns in the center and on the kingside, with a pawn on e4 and another on f3, both potentially supporting central control. My position is relatively undeveloped, though I have a strong queen on d8 and a bishop on c8 that can become active. The white king appears to have already moved (KWF1), so castling rights are likely lost for White. My pawn on d5 is under attack by White’s pawn on e4, and I must respond carefully to avoid losing material.\n\nThe most pressing issues are:\n- The d5 pawn is attacked by e4 and must either move or be defended.\n- Development of minor pieces (knights, bishops) is lagging.\n- Potential to open lines with pawn breaks like ...f5 or ...c5.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: d5-d4 – Pushing the pawn forward challenges e4 and gains space, but may overextend if not supported.\n- P: d5-c4 – Capturing would remove the defender of e4, but opens the b-file and could weaken my structure.\n- P: f7-f5 – A solid developing break, preparing to develop the bishop to f6 or g7, and contesting the center.\n- B: c8-g4 – Developing the bishop actively, pinning a knight if Nf3 were present, but currently no such knight exists.\n- B: c8-e6 – A flexible development square, eyeing the center and avoiding early pin ideas.\n- Q: d8-d7 – Connecting rooks or preparing queenside castling (if allowed), though king safety is questionable.\n- N: b8-c6 – Developing the knight centrally, supporting a potential ...e5 or defending d4.\n- P: f7-f6 – A defensive move, reinforcing e5, but somewhat passive.\n\nAmong these, P: f7-f5 stands out as it immediately stakes a claim in the center, opens diagonals for the bishop and queen, and prepares kingside development. It avoids immediate tactical vulnerability and increases pressure.\n\nReason: Playing P: f7-f5 asserts control over the e4 square, discourages further White advances like d4, and facilitates quick development of the kingside bishop and eventual castling. It also prevents White from easily expanding with d2-d4. Compared to other options, it is more dynamic and less committal than capturing on c4 or pushing to d4 prematurely.\n\nAnswer: P: f7-f5"}
