es through c3, d4, e5, f6 — not f3.\n\nSo **Qb2 does not attack f3**.\n\nThus, **Qf6+ is not followed by Qxf3+** — impossible.\n\nSo Qb2-f6+ checks the king via the diagonal: b2–c3–d4–e5–f6.\n\nAfter Qf6+, king must go to c1, d2, or e1.\n\nThen what? No immediate follow-up.\n\nBut still, it pressures the center and may win time.\n\nBetter idea: **P: e4-f3** — is that a valid move?\n\nYes: **P: e4-f3** is in the list.\n\ne4 has PBF1 (Black pawn), f3 has PWF1 (White pawn). But pawns capture diagonally — e4 to f3 is forward-left diagonal? From Black’s perspective: e4 is rank 4, f3 is rank 3 — so going down — but Black pawns move *down* the board (from rank 7 to 1), so from e4 to f3 is *forward* (rank decreases) and file increases — so it’s a **diagonal forward move** — i.e., a capture.\n\nYes — so **P: e4-f3** captures the pawn on f3.\n\nIs it protected? f3 is defended by e3-pawn (PWF1 on e3).\n\nSo after ...exf3, White plays gxf3 — but then **Qb2-b1+!** or **Qb2-e5+**, but not necessarily strong.\n\nWait — better: after exf3, gxf3, then **Qb2-b1+!**\n\nBecause b1 has NWF0 (White knight) — so queen checks on b1, attacking the knight.\n\nWhite must:\n- Move king → Kb2? But b2 is where queen came from — king on d1, can it go to c2? c2 has PWF0 — occupied.\n\nKing moves: c1, d2, e1, e2 (but e2 has bishop), c2 blocked.\n\nSo only c1, d2, e1.\n\nSay Kc1 — then Qxb1+ wins the knight.\n\nYes! So:\n\n1. ... e4xf3\n2. g2xf3 (forced, otherwise loses g-pawn and opens g-file)\n   - If not, say gxh3? No g-file connection.\n   - Actually, White must recapture with g-pawn? No — only if g-pawn is adjacent.\n\ng-pawn: g3 has PWF1 — yes, g3 pawn is adjacent to f3.\n\nSo gxf3 is natural.\n\nThen:\n2. ... Qb2-b1+\n3. Kd1-c2? But c2 has PWF0 → no\n   - Kd1-c1 → then Qxb1 wins knight\n   - Kd1-e1 → Qb1+ still wins knight\n   - Kd1-d2 → Qb1+ again wins knight\n\nAll king moves allow Qxb1 — since b1 is attacked and no protection.\n\nSo **winning a piece** — a full knight — for just a pawn.\n\