ur own piece.\n\nWait — after Kxe2, the king is on e2. My queen is on b2. Can I play Qb2-e2? That would be capturing the king? No — in chess, you move to the square of the enemy piece.\n\nBut after Kxe2, the king is on e2 — so if I play Qb2-e2, I would be moving my queen to e2 — which is occupied by the enemy king — that’s capturing the king — which is checkmate if he can’t escape.\n\nBut is it check? Yes — queen attacks e2.\n\nCan the king escape? From e2, king can go to d1, d2, d3, e1, e3, f1, f2, f3.\n\nBut:\n- d1: attacked by queen? Qb2 to d1 — is that diagonal? b2 to d1: b2-c1-d1? Not straight — no. b2 to d1 is two right, one down — not diagonal. So not attacked.\n- d2: pawn on d2 — PWF0 — so occupied.\n- d3: empty?\n- e1: empty?\n- e3: empty?\n- f1: empty?\n- f2: empty\n- f3: empty (was f3, now pawn captured)\n\nBut queen on e2 controls:\n- Entire e-file\n- Entire 2nd rank\n- Diagonals: from e2 — a6, b5, c4, d3, f1, g4, h5; and opposite: d1, c2, b3, a4; f3, g4, h5.\n\nSo after Qe2+, king on e2 is in check.\n\nWhere can it go?\n\n- d1: is it attacked? Queen on e2 — e2 to d1 is diagonal — yes, one square southwest — so yes, d1 is attacked.\n- d2: occupied by White pawn\n- d3: attacked by queen (diagonal e2-d3)\n- e1: attacked by queen (same file)\n- e3: attacked by queen (same file)\n- f1: attacked by queen (diagonal e2-f1)\n- f2: attacked by queen (same rank)\n- f3: attacked by queen (diagonal e2-f3)\n\nAll eight surrounding squares are either attacked or occupied.\n\n- d1: attacked\n- d2: occupied (pawn)\n- d3: attacked\n- e1: attacked\n- e3: attacked\n- f1: attacked\n- f2: attacked\n- f3: attacked\n\nAnd the king has no piece to interpose — it\'s a queen check.\n\nTherefore — **after Qb2-e2+, it\'s checkmate**.\n\nBut can I play Qb2-e2 after fxe2 and Kxe2?\n\nYes — provided it\'s my next move.\n\nSo the sequence:\n1. ... P: f3-e2 (capture bishop)\n   - Now, White is in a dilemma: if Kxe2, then Qb2-e2# — mate.\n   - So White must avoid Kxe2.\n\nWhat other options does White have after ...fxe2?\n\n- Recapture with another piece? No — only king is near.\n- Block? Impossible — it\'s a capture, not a check.\n- Move king without capturing? But the bishop is captured — so White must either recapture or leave it.\n\nBut if White doesn’t recapture, I just have an extra bishop — even better.\n\nSo after ...fxe2, White loses at least a piece.\n\nIf Kxe2, Qe2# — instant loss.\n\nTherefore, White cannot recapture — so I win the bishop for free.\n\nMoreover, the threat of Qe2+ forces White to abandon the king’s safety.\n\nTherefore, **P: f3-e2** is crushing.\n\nNo other move comes close.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **P: f3-e2**: Wins material with mating threat; highly favorable.\n- **d5-d4**: Solid, but slow in comparison.\n- **N: b8-c6**: Developing, but ignores immediate tactics.\n- **Qb2-e5**: Not listed — not allowed.\n\nReason: P: f3-e2 creates an immediate tactical blow, exploiting the undefended bishop on e2 and the alignment of my queen on b2 with the e-file. After capturing the bishop, White cannot recapture with the king due to the threat of Qe2#. This wins at least a piece and severely damages White’s position.\n\nAnswer: P: f3-e2'}
