s, but not immediately threatening.\n\nBack to threats.\n\nThe most urgent issue: **White has a passed pawn on c5**, deep in black\'s side. If unchallenged, it could advance to c6-c7-c8 and promote. We must stop it.\n\nHow can Black challenge it?\n\n- Black pawn on d4 (PBF1) cannot take c5 directly — it would require capturing diagonally, but d4 to c5 is backward? d4 to c5 is one square forward-left? From Black’s perspective, moving up the board is toward lower ranks? No!\n\nWait: **Crucial correction**:\n\nIn standard chess:\n\n- Black starts on ranks 8 and 7.\n- Black moves **downward**, toward rank 1.\n- So for a Black pawn, "forward" is **decreasing rank number**.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- A Black pawn on d4 (rank 4) moves toward d3, d2, etc.\n- The white pawn on c5 is on rank 5 — so from Black’s view, it\'s **above** d4.\n\nCan the Black pawn on d4 capture the pawn on c5? Only if it\'s diagonally forward.\n\nFrom d4, diagonally forward for Black is to c3 or e3 — not c5. c5 is **up and left**, but from d4 to c5 is **increasing rank** — that\'s **backward** for Black.\n\nSo **no**, the d4 pawn cannot capture c5. In fact, the c5 pawn is **behind** the d4 pawn from Black’s perspective.\n\nMoreover, the c5 pawn is isolated and potentially vulnerable.\n\nBut it can advance: c5→c6→c7, and if not stopped, promote.\n\nWho can stop it?\n\n- Black knight on b8 can go to c6 — but that\'s under attack by whom? c6 is not currently attacked.\n- Or knight to a6, c6, d7.\n\nBut more pressing: **Black has a powerful queen on c1**, sitting deep in White’s camp, threatening back-rank damage.\n\nc1 is on White’s first rank. The White king is on f2 — very exposed.\n\nFrom c1, the Black queen can go to c2, c3, c4, c5? c5 has a white pawn — can we capture it?\n\nQ: c1-c5? Is that in the valid moves list?\n\nValid moves: Q: c1-c2, c1-c3, c1-c4, c1-c5 — yes! **Q: c1-c5** is listed.\n\nAnd c5 has PWF1 — a white pawn. So **capturing on c5 is possible**.\n\nIs it safe? After Qxc5, can White recap