ther moves like ...g5 or ...h5 are flank moves, less effective.\n\nKnights: N: b8-c6 — same idea, but pawn first may be better.\n\nDeveloping knight is good, but pawn move fights for space.\n\nNow, evaluate **P: c7-c6** vs **N: b8-c6**.\n\nIf we play knight first, White can play c5-c6, winning a pawn — because knight cannot stay on c6 if pawn pushes.\n\nFor example: 1...Nc6? 2.c5-c6! — now the pawn captures — but c5 to c6 is forward? For White, forward is increasing rank? No!\n\nCritical: White pawns move **up** the board — from rank 2 to 8.\n\nSo White pawn on c5 (rank 5) moves to c6 (rank 6), then c7, c8.\n\nSo c5-c6 is a legal move.\n\nIf Black knight is on c6, and White plays c5-c6, it captures the knight — because c5 to c6 is straight forward, not diagonal.\n\nNo: pawn captures diagonally.\n\nA pawn on c5 captures to b6 or d6 — not c6.\n\nc6 is directly ahead — so pawn moves c5-c6 without capturing.\n\nSo if Black plays Nc6, and White plays c5-c6, then the pawn **displaces** the knight — but only if the knight is on c6.\n\nBut a pawn cannot “displace” — it can only move to an empty square.\n\nSo if c6 is occupied, pawn cannot advance.\n\nTherefore, if Black plays **Nc6**, it **blocks** the c5 pawn from advancing to c6.\n\nSimilarly, if Black plays **c6**, then c5-c6 is impossible because c6 is occupied.\n\nSo both moves block the pawn.\n\nBut **c6** is better because:\n\n- It’s a pawn move, so it permanently fixes the structure.\n- It avoids placing the knight on a square where it might be harassed.\n- After c6, the knight can go to d7 or e7, flexible.\n\nWhereas Nc6 blocks but uses a piece.\n\nAlso, c6 supports a future ...d5.\n\nSo **P: c7-c6** seems strong.\n\nIs there any risk? After c6, does White have tactics?\n\nFor example, c5-c6? No — c6 occupied.\n\nOr b2-b4? But no b2 pawn.\n\nNo immediate tactic.\n\nAlternatively, **P: b7-b5**? To challenge c5? b5 attacks c4, not c5.\n\nNo.\n\nSo **P: c7-c6** is a solid, principled move.\n\nAnother option: **P: a7-a