ry strong — **B:c5-b4** attacks the undefended knight on d2 directly.\n- B: c5-a3 – Also targets d2 via a3, but longer route; less direct.\n- B: c5-d4 – Central square, but doesn’t attack anything immediately.\n- K: g8-f7/g7/h8/f8 – King moves are unnecessary and waste time in this position.\n- P: b7-b5 – Gains space, prepares ...b4, but opens the a8-h1 diagonal slightly for White’s pieces.\n- P: h7-h5/h6 – Very slow, no urgency on the kingside.\n\nCritical observation: **The white knight on d2 (NWF1) is hanging** — it is attacked by the black bishop on c5 and is not defended. Therefore, playing **B: c5-b4** wins material immediately.\n\nReason: **B: c5-b4** captures the undefended knight on d2 (though notation shows only src-dst, the move implies capture). It gains a full piece (knight) for free, which is decisive in most games. No other move offers such a concrete advantage.\n\nAnswer: B: c5-b4"}
