n h4 → Black Bishop on h4\n\nSo yes: **I can play B: h4-g5**, delivering check to the White king on g4.\n\nLet’s verify if that’s legal and safe:\n\n- B: h4-g5 — moves from h4 to g5, which is one diagonal step — valid.\n- After B: h4-g5, the White king on g4 will be under attack (in check).\n- Can the White king escape? It can go to f4, f5, g5 (blocked), h5, h4 (but h4 is now vacated), or g3?\n  - But after our move, g5 is occupied by our bishop.\n  - Possible escapes: f4, f5, h5, h4, g3 — but we must see if those are available.\n- However, even if the king can escape, **we are giving check**, and possibly setting up a winning attack.\n\nBut wait — is this checkmate?\n\nAfter **B: h4-g5+**, White king on g4 is attacked.\n\nPossible escape squares:\n\n- f4 — is it attacked? Our rook is on f5 — yes! Rf5 attacks f4 (same file). So f4 is covered.\n- f5 — occupied by our own rook (RBF1 on f5) — so no.\n- g5 — occupied by our bishop.\n- h4 — empty? Yes, but is it safe? King moves to h4 — is it attacked?\n  - Our bishop was on h4, now moved — but does anything attack h4?\n  - Our rook on f5 doesn't attack h4.\n  - Our knight on a6? No.\n  - Our pawns? No.\n  - So h4 is *not* attacked — so White king could go to h4?\n  - But wait: is h4 accessible? From g4 to h4 is horizontal — yes, one square.\n\nSo king could escape to h4.\n\nBut wait: is h4 protected? After we play B: h4-g5, the square h4 is empty. King moves from g4 to h4 — is that legal?\n\nYes — unless h4 is attacked.\n\nIs any Black piece attacking h4?\n\n- Rook on f5: controls rank 5 — h4 is rank 4 → no.\n- Knight on a6: too far.\n- Bishop on g5 (our new position) — it’s on g5, which is diagonal from h4? g5 to h4 is one diagonal — yes, so **bishop on g5 attacks h4**.\n\nBecause g5 to h4 is a diagonal (southeast), yes — same color diagonal.\n\nTherefore, **after B: h4-g5, the bishop on g5 attacks both g4 (where king is) and h4 (escape square)**.\n\nNow check other escape squares:\n\n- f4: attacked by rook on f5 (same file) — covered.\n- f5: occupied by Black rook — not available.\n- g5: occupied by our bishop.\n- h5: is it available? h5 is one square above h4. Is it attacked?\n  - Our bishop on g5 → can it attack h5? g5 to h5 is one square right — horizontal, not diagonal. So no.\n  - Rook on f5: controls rank 5 — yes, h5 is on rank 5 → rook on f5 attacks h5.\n  - Therefore, h5 is attacked.\n\nSo all escape squares:\n\n- f4 → attacked by rook\n- f5 → occupied\n- g5 → occupied\n- h5 → attacked by rook\n- h4 → attacked by bishop on g5\n\nTherefore, **White king has no legal moves** — and is in check.\n\nThus, **B: h4-g5 is checkmate**.\n\nEvaluation:\n- B: h4-g5: delivers checkmate — immediate win.\n- Other moves like moving the knight or pawn are irrelevant now — there’s a forced mate.\n- Also, R: f5-f4+ would also be check, but let’s see: Rf5 to f4 attacks g4 — yes, also check. But then king can go to h4 or h5? h5 might be safer? But not necessarily mate.\n  - After R: f5-f4+, king can go to h4 — not attacked by anything unless we cover it. But we don’t. So not mate.\n  - But with bishop move, we cut off h4.\n\nOnly B: h4-g5 cuts off both g4 and h4 while checking.\n\nMoreover, is the bishop move safe? Is the bishop on h4 hanging? No — White cannot move before us. We are to move.\n\nTherefore, **B: h4-g5 is checkmate**.\n\nReason: The move B: h4-g5 places the White king in check with no legal escape squares, as all surrounding squares (f4, f5, g5, h4, h5) are either occupied, blocked, or attacked. The rook on f5 controls f4 and h5, and the bishop on g5 controls g4 and h4, resulting in checkmate.\n\nAnswer: B: h4-g5"}
