5 (odd) → dark — yes.\n\nc5 is currently empty.\n\nIs c5 a good square? Yes — central, long diagonal to f8, and pressures d4, e3, f2, g1 — but more importantly, **it attacks the white king on f4 along the diagonal?**\n\nFrom c5 to f4: c5 → d4 → e3 → f2 → g1 — that’s the c5-g1 diagonal? c5 to f4: Δfile=+3, Δrank=-1 — not diagonal. Diagonals require equal Δfile and Δrank.\n\nc5 to f4: file +3, rank -1 — not equal, so not diagonal.\n\nSo no.\n\nBut c5 is still a strong square.\n\nBut **best immediate idea**: the white king is on f4 — a light square.\n\nOur bishop is on f2 — dark-squared bishop.\n\nIt cannot attack f4.\n\nBut perhaps we can prepare a trap.\n\nWait — what if we play **N: e4-g3**?\n\ng3 is empty.\n\nFrom g3, the knight attacks f1, h1, f5, h5, e2, e4.\n\nf4 is adjacent — but not attacked.\n\nBut if the white king moves to g5 or e5, we might respond.\n\nAlternatively, **N: e4-d6** — d6 is dark, knight jumps.\n\nBut d6 is close to c8, where our king is.\n\nBut no clear threat.\n\nLet’s reconsider captures.\n\nCan we play **b7-a6**? Yes — capture white pawn on a6.\n\nThis wins a pawn — material advantage.\n\nCurrently, material:\n- White has: knight on f7, bishop on f2, pawns on a6, d3, h3, and king\n- Black has: knight on e4, bishop on f2 (ours), pawns on b7, h7, and king\n\nAfter b7-a6: we capture PWF1 on a6 — so we remove a white pawn.\n\nThen we have a passed pawn on a6?\n\nBut white might recapture with b2 or something — but no white pawn near.\n\nWhite pawns: a6 was their only one on a-file? They have d3 and h3.\n\nSo after ...b7xa6, the a-file is open, and we can potentially push a6-a5-a4, etc.\n\nBut slow.\n\nAlternatively, **N: e4-c5** forks a6 and d3? From c5, knight attacks a6? c5 to a6: Δfile=-2, Δrank=+1 — yes, that’s a knight move.\n\nAnd c5 to d3: Δfile=+1, Δrank=-2 — yes.\n\nSo **N: e4-c5** attacks both a6 and d3 — a fork!\n\na6 has PWF1 (white pawn), d3 has PWF1 (white pawn) — both are enemy pieces.\n\nSo after N: e4-c5, next turn we can play