\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: b7-b5, P: b7-a6, P: b7-b6, N: c5-a4, N: c5-a6, N: c5-b3, N: c5-d3, N: c5-d7, N: c5-e4, N: c5-e6, B: d4-c3, B: d4-b2, B: d4-a1, B: d4-e3, B: d4-f2, B: d4-g1, B: d4-e5, B: d4-f6, B: d4-g7, B: d4-h8, K: g8-f7, K: g8-f8, K: g8-g7, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and my king is on g8 (KBF1), having already moved, so castling is not possible. White’s king is on f3 (KWF1), also having moved. There is a dangerous white knight on f7 (NWF1), which is just one square away from delivering a potential fork or even checkmate threats, especially targeting the black king on g8 and possibly other valuable pieces. My material includes two pawns on b7 and h7, a bishop on d4 (BBF1), and a knight on c5 (NBF1). The position is relatively open, and development appears uneven.\n\nWhite has a pawn on e6 (PWF1) and another on h4 (PWF1), both potentially threatening. The white knight on f7 is deep in my territory and could be a major threat if not addressed. However, it is currently undefended — this is critical. It can be captured immediately if I act.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **N: c5-a4 / N: c5-a6 / N: c5-b3 / N: c5-d3 / N: c5-d7 / N: c5-e4 / N: c5-e6**: These knight moves develop or reposition the knight, but none address the immediate tactical opportunity.\n- **B: d4-c3 / B: d4-b2 / B: d4-a1 / B: d4-e3 / B: d4-f2 / B: d4-g1 / B: d4-e5 / B: d4-f6 / B: d4-g7 / B: d4-h8**: Most of these bishop moves are passive or slow. B: d4-g7 would attack the knight on f7, but it's not a capture — it just attacks. However, note that **B: d4-g7** puts the bishop on g7, eyeing the f7-square, but doesn't take immediately.\n- **K: g8-f7**: This is **king capturing the knight on f7**! The white knight on f7 is undefended, and the black king can legally move to f7 (g8 → f7), capturing it. This is a free piece! The square f7 is not attacked by any white piece — white has nothing on e6, g6, or e8/g8 to threaten f7. So this capture is safe.\n- **P: b7-b5 / b7-b6 / b7-a6 / h7-h5 / h7-h6**: Pawn moves are slow and do not capitalize on the tactical blunder.\n\nReason: The most forcing and advantageous move is **K: g8-f7**, capturing the undefended white knight on f7. This gains a full piece (knight) for no cost, significantly increasing Black's material advantage. The king is not placed in check after the move — f7 is safe. This is a high-value, low-risk move that should be taken immediately before White realizes the mistake and defends or moves the knight.\n\nAnswer: K: g8-f7"}
