\n1 |    |    |    |KWF1|    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[N: b5-a3, N: b5-c3, N: b5-c7, N: b5-d4, N: b5-d6, P: b7-a6, P: b7-b6, K: f7-e6, K: f7-e7, K: f7-e8, K: f7-f6, K: f7-f8, K: f7-g8, B: g7-f6, B: g7-e5, B: g7-d4, B: g7-c3, B: g7-b2, B: g7-a1, B: g7-h6, B: g7-f8, B: g7-h8, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as Black, and my position is precarious. My king on f7 is exposed and currently under threat from the white pawn on d3 (though not immediately in check). White has a dangerous advanced pawn on b6 (PWF1), just one square away from promotion if unimpeded. My knight on b5 is active but lacks immediate tactical targets. My bishop on g7 has long diagonal potential but is somewhat blocked. My own pawns are scattered, with limited mobility.\n\nKey concerns:\n- The white pawn on b6 is a major threat — it can promote next move if unchecked (b6-b7 or b6-b8=Q).\n- My knight on b5 can potentially disrupt central control or prevent promotion.\n- My king needs to stay safe but also may need to become active.\n- Immediate counterplay is necessary to avoid falling into a lost endgame due to white’s passed pawn.\n\nEvaluation:\n1. **N: b5-a3** – Attacks the b-pawn indirectly by eyeing b1, but removes knight from central influence; slow.\n2. **N: b5-c3** – Threatens fork ideas and attacks the b-pawn directly. Strong candidate, as it captures the advanced white pawn next turn if not defended.\n3. **N: b5-c7** – Moves toward the center, possibly supporting ...Nb5-d5 or ...Nc7-e6, but passive in fa