s down: that\'s a diagonal of 2√2 — yes, bishop move. And a3 is on the same diagonal as c5? c5 → b4 → a3: yes! So both b4 and a3 are check options.\n  - However, only one of them is correct based on board geometry.\n  - Bishop on c5: to reach b4 is c5→b4 (one left, one up) — but b4 is rank 4, file b — that’s one up, one left — yes, valid.\n  - To reach a3: c5→b4→a3 — two left, two up? No: c5 to a3 is two left and two down? Rank 5 to rank 3 is down two. Files c to a is left two. So direction is left-down diagonal. Yes — bishop moves exactly on that. So both b4 and a3 are valid and both give check?\n  - Wait: from c5, moving to b4 is up-left (file c→b, rank 5→4)? No: rank 5 to 4 is down. So c5 to b4 is left and down — that’s toward a3, not away. So:\n    - c5 to b4: file -1, rank -1 → d4 would be +1 file, -1 rank? Let\'s clarify coordinates.\n\nLet’s fix coordinate system:\n\n- File: a=1, b=2, c=3, d=4, e=5, f=6, g=7, h=8\n- Rank: 1 (bottom) to 8 (top)\n\nSo:\n- c5 = (3,5)\n- b4 = (2,4) → difference (-1,-1) → valid bishop move\n- a3 = (1,3) → difference (-2,-2) → also valid bishop move\n\nAnd king is at c3 = (3,3)\n\nNow: does Bc5-b4 give check? After move, bishop at b4=(2,4). Distance to king at (3,3): Δfile=+1, Δrank=-1 → yes, diagonal, so yes — b4 attacks c3.\n\nSimilarly, Ba3=(1,3): distance to c3=(3,3): Δfile=+2, Δrank=0 → horizontal, not diagonal. So Ba3 does NOT attack c3.\n\nWait! That’s critical.\n\nFrom c5 to a3: moves along the diagonal from (3,5) → (2,4) → (1,3). That’s the a3-c5 diagonal. But c3 is at (3,3), which is below c5, not on that line.\n\nThe diagonal from c5 going down-left goes through b4, a3 — decreasing both file and rank.\n\nBut c3 is same file as c5, but lower rank — so it’s on the vertical.\n\nSo bishop at a3 does NOT attack c3. Only squares on the diagonals from a3: b4, c5, etc. (up-right) or zilch down-left.\n\nSo Ba3 does NOT check the king.\n\nBut Bb4 does: from b4=(2,4) to c3=(3,3): Δfile=+1, Δrank=-1 → yes, same diagonal. So Bc5-b4 gives check.\n\nSimilarly, other bishop moves like c5-d4=(4,4): to c3=(3,3): Δfile=-1, Δrank=-1 → yes, also diagonal. So Bd4 also attacks c3?\n\nd4=(4,4), c3=(3,3): yes, Δ(-1,-1) → same diagonal. So Bc5-d4 also gives check!\n\nWait — really?\n\nc5 to d4: (3,5) → (4,4): Δ(+1,-1) → valid bishop move.\n\nThen from d4 to c3: (4,4)→(3,3): Δ(-1,-1) → yes, same diagonal. So d4 is also on the diagonal path to c3.\n\nBut is the bishop attacking c3 from d4? Yes — so Bc5-d4 also delivers check.\n\nSimilarly, c5-e7? e7=(5,7): that’s up-right — far from king.\n\nBut let\'s list all bishop moves from c5 that land on the diagonal passing through c3.\n\nThe key diagonal is the one from top-left to bottom-right: a1-h8? No — c3 and b4 and a5? a5 is black.\n\nWait: c3=(3,3), b4=(2,4), a5=(1,5) — that’s one diagonal: increasing rank as file decreases.\n\nBut our bishop is at c5=(3,5). From c5, going down-left: b4=(2,4), a3=(1,3) — this is a different diagonal: slope = (+1,+1) in (file,rank)? No.\n\nActually, the diagonal with constant (file + rank):\n\n- c5: 3+5=8\n- b4: 2+4=6 → different\n- a3: 1+3=4\n- d4: 4+4=8 → same as c5\n- e3: 5+3=8 → yes\n- f2: 6+2=8\n- g1: 7+1=8\n\nAh! So the diagonal where file + rank = 8.\n\nc5: 3+5=8 → yes  \nd4: 4+4=8 → yes  \ne3: 5+3=8 → yes  \nf2: 6+2=8 → yes  \ng1: 7+1=8 → yes  \n\nAnd c3: 3+3=6 → not on that diagonal.\n\nBut earlier we thought b4 attacks c3? b4=(2,4), c3=(3,3): 2+4=6, 3+3=6 → both sum to 6 → yes, they are on the diagonal file+rank=6.\n\nSo:\n- Diagonal file+rank=6: includes a5, b4, c3, d2, e1\n- Diagonal file+rank=8: includes c5, d4, e3, f2, g1\n\nTherefore:\n- Bishop on c5 is on file+rank=8 diagonal\n- King on c3 is on file+rank=6 diagonal\n- These are parallel, never intersect\n\nSo bishop on c5 cannot attack c3 via any move?\n\nWait — then how could Bc5-b4 attack c3?\n\nb4=(2,4): file+rank=6  \nc3=(3,3): file+rank=6 → same diagonal → yes, so b4 and c3 are on same diagonal.\n\nBut c5=(3,5): file+rank=8 → different.\n\nSo when bishop moves from c5 to b4, it changes diagonals.\n\nc5 to b4: (3,5)→(2,4): Δ(-1,-1) → this is along the file+rank=8? 3+5=8, 2+4=6 → no, 8 to 6 — not same.\n\nWait: (-1,-1) means it\'s moving along a diagonal where file - rank is constant?\n\nc5: 3-5 = -2  \nb4: 2-4 = -2 → yes!  \na3: 1-3 = -2 → yes  \nd4: 4-4=0 → no\n\nSo the diagonal where file - rank = -2: c5, b4, a3\n\nNow, c3: 3-3=0 → not on that.\n\nBut b4=(2,4): 2-4=-2  \nc3=(3,3): 3-3=0 → different\n\nDo they align on another diagonal?\n\nFor bishop to attack from b4 to c3: need same |Δfile| = |Δrank| and continuous.\n\nFrom b4 to c3: file +1, rank -1 → |+1|=|-1| → yes, and it\'s a diagonal move.\n\nWhich diagonal? file + rank: b4=6, c3=6 → yes! Both sum to 6.\n\nSo b4 is on two diagonals:\n- file - rank = -2 (from c5 to a3)\n- file + rank = 6 (to c3, a5, etc.)\n\nSo yes — b4 is at intersection.\n\nThus, bishop moving to b4 can attack c3 because b4 and c3 share the file+rank=6 diagonal.\n\nSimilarly, what about d4?\n\nd4=(4,4): file+rank=8, file-rank=0\n\nTo c3=(3,3): file+rank=6, file-rank=0 → same file-rank (0), so yes, on same diagonal (the main a1-h8 type).\n\nfile-rank: d4=0, c3=0 → yes.\n\nSo d4 and c3 are on same diagonal (file - rank = 0), which includes a1, b2, c3, d4, e5, f6, g7, h8.\n\nYes! So d4 is on the long diagonal.\n\nTherefore, bishop from c5 to d4: (3,5)→(4,4): Δ(+1,-1) → valid, and lands on d4, which is on diagonal with c3.\n\nSo Bd4 also attacks c3.\n\nSimilarly, can it go to e5? e5 is occupied by NBF1.\n\nBut e5=(5,5): file-rank=0 → yes, but c5 to e5 is not diagonal.\n\nAnyway, back: both **B: c5-b4** and **B: c5-d4** deliver check to the white king at c3.\n\nAre there others?\n\nWhat about **B: c5-e7**? e7=(5,7): too far, no relation.\n\nOr **N: e5-c4**? c4=(3,4): to c3=(3,3): adjacent, so knight at c4 attacks c3? Knight moves in L-shape.\n\nc4 to c3: straight down — not L-shape. Invalid.\n\nc4 to b2: (-1,-2) — yes, that’s a knight move, but not to c3.\n\nKnight on e5=(5,5): to c4=(3,4): Δ(-2,-1) — yes, valid knight move.\n\nFrom c4, does it attack c3? No — knight doesn’t attack adjacent squares.\n\nOnly possible knight checks would be if it lands on a square that attacks c3.\n\nSquares that attack c3 via knight move: \n- a2: (1,2) → to c3: +2,+1\n- b1: (2,1) → +1,+2\n- d1: (4,1) → -1,+2\n- e2: (5,2) → -2,+1\n- e4: (5,4) → -2,-1? To c3: (5,4)→(3,3): Δ(-2,-1) — yes\n- d5: (4,5) → -1,-2\n- b5: (2,5) → +1,-2\n- a4: (1,4) → +2,-1\n\nNone of these are reachable from e5 in one move, except possibly:\n- e5 to c4: (5,5)→(3,4): Δ(-2,-1) — yes, but c4 does not attack c3\n- e5 to d3: (5,5)→(4,3): Δ(-1,-2) — yes, d3=(4,3)\n  - d3 to c3: left one — not knight move\n- e5 to f3: (5,5)→(6,3): Δ(+1,-2) — f3=(6,3)\n  - f3 to c3: too far\n- e5 to g4: (7,4)\n- e5 to g6: (7,6)\n- e5 to e7: (5,7)\n- e5 to c6: (3,6)\n\nNone of these knight destinations attack c3.\n\nSo only the bishop moves **c5-b4** and **c5-d4** give check.\n\nAdditionally, **B: c5-a3**? a3=(1,3): from c5=(3,5): Δ(-2,-2) → yes, valid bishop move.\n\nBut a3 to c3: (1,3)→(3,3): Δ(+2,0) → horizontal, not diagonal → no attack.\n\nSo Ba3 does not check.\n\nSimilarly, **B: c5-b6**? b6=(2,6): Δ(-1,+1) → yes, but b6 to c3? No.\n\nSo only two checking moves: **B: c5-b4** and **B: c5-d4**\n\nNow, between them:\n- **B: c5-b4**: places bishop on b4, attacking king at c3. Is it protected? Currently, no black piece defends b4. Can white capture it? What captures on b4?\n  - White has pawns: b2 pawn — but it moved already (PWF1), and it\'s on b2. Can it capture on b4? No — pawn captures diagonally forward. From b2, it can capture on a3 or c3 — not b4.\n  - Other pieces? White has no other pieces apparent. So b4 may be safe.\n  - But after Bb4+, white must respond: either move king, block, or capture.\n  - King on c3: can move to b3, b2, c2, d2, d3, d4, c4, b4? But b4 is attacked.\n  - Possible escapes: b3, b2, c2, d2, d3, d4, c4 — but need to see which are available.\n  - For example, d4: is it attacked? Not yet. But after