c., don't challenge b8.\n\nBut **Nc6 is the only move that directly threatens to remove the newly promoted queen**.\n\nEven better: **is b8 attacked by anything else? No. Only the knight can go there soon**.\n\nAnother idea: **can I play Kb8? But king is on f7 — too far**.\n\nSo **Nc6 is the best practical chance** — it forces simplification and may save a draw.\n\nBut let’s check: after Nc6, does White have to allow the trade?\n\nThey can play b8=R or b8=B or b8=N to avoid losing the piece? But even if they underpromote, say to knight, I still capture — Nc6 x b8, and I’m trading knight for knight — but they spent a pawn to get a knight — bad for them. Or if they promote to bishop, same thing — I capture, they get nothing.\n\nOnly if they promote to queen or rook, I capture and get full value.\n\nBut regardless, **they gain little** — at best, a knight trade where they spent a pawn — bad.\n\nOr if they don’t promote, they leave the pawn on b7 — but why wouldn’t they? They want to win.\n\nBut perhaps they play something else? Like Kd4-e5? But no — their priority is to promote.\n\nSo they will promote, I capture — resulting in likely drawn endgame.\n\nAny other knight move doesn’t contest b8.\n\nFor example, Nd3: attacks d4 king — but White can play b8=Q first, then deal with check.\n\nNd3 gives check? From d3 to d4 — adjacent? d3 and d4 — yes, knight on d3 attacks c1, e1, b2, f2, c5, e5, b4, f4 — not d4. Knight doesn’t attack adjacent squares. So Nd3 does not check the king.\n\nd4 is king, d3 is one below — knight on d3 attacks:\n- d3+2,+1 = d5+e → e5\n- d3+2,-1 = d5+c → c5\n- d3-2,+1 = d1+e → e1\n- d3-2,-1 = d1+c → c1\n- d3+1,+2 = d4+f → f4\n- d3+1,-2 = d4+b → b4\n- d3-1,+2 = d2+f → f2\n- d3-1,-2 = d2+b → b2\n\nSo no — does not attack d4. So no check.\n\nThus, no forcing move.\n\nOnly **Nc6** creates a tangible threat.\n\nAnother possibility: **Kf7-e8** or **Ke7**, centralizing, but too slow.\n\nSo **N: e5-c6** is the best move — it challenges the queening square and offers drawing chances by forcing simplification.\n\nReason: It is the only move that contests the b8 square, threatening to immediately capture any piece White promotes on b8, thereby neutralizing White’s impending promotion and reducing the position to a likely drawn endgame.\n\nAnswer: N: e5-c6"}
