\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a5-a4, K: f7-e6, K: f7-e7, K: f7-e8, K: f7-f6, K: f7-f8, K: f7-g6, K: f7-g8, N: g7-e6, N: g7-e8, N: g7-f5, N: g7-h5]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and my king is on f7, with a knight on g7 and pawns on f7 (no, wait — pawn on h7 and knight on g7). White has a king on c6, which is deep in my territory, and a pawn on h6. My position is extremely precarious: my king is near the center and at risk of being mated soon. The white king on c6 is close to promoting or supporting its pawn, but currently it's my turn. However, my king is not in check yet. I must make a safe and improving move.\n\nLooking at the board:\n- My king on f7 has several moves: e8, e7, e6, f6, f8, g6, g8.\n- My knight on g7 can go to e6, e8, f5, or h5.\n- My only pawn on a5 can play a5-a4.\n\nThe white king on c6 is very active and threatening to march toward the queenside or support its own pawn. My pieces are undeveloped and cramped. I need to activate my king or develop counterplay.\n\nEvaluation:\n1. **K: f7-e6** – Centralizes the king, approaching the center. But this walks toward the white king (c6), possibly leading to an unfavorable king confrontation. Also, e6 may be attacked later by White’s pieces. Risky.\n\n2. **K: f7-e7** – Retreats slightly, connecting with potential back-rank safety. Keeps king in the center file. Modest improvement.\n\n3. **K: f7-e8** – Moves king to e8, safer on the back rank. Prepares for potential rook activation (if I had one), but I don’t. Somewhat passive.\n\n4. **K: f7-f8** – Similar idea, keeps king on the edge. Less central than e8.\n\n5. **K: f7-g8** – Heads toward kingside, possibly to support h7 pawn or prepare ...h6. A bit slow.\n\n6. **N: g7-e6** – Develops knight to a central square. Blocks potential white king advance to d5 or e5. Could become active. Good developing move.\n\n7. **N: g7-e8** – Retreats knight, awkward.\n\n8. **N: g7-f5** – Knight jumps to f5, a strong outpost. Controls key central squares (e.g., e7, d6, g7, h6). Puts pressure on White’s position. Very active.\n\n9. **N: g7-h5** – Edge move, less useful unless targeting g3 or f4 later. Not urgent.\n\n10. **P: a5-a4** – Prevents b2-b4 ideas (but no white pawn on b2), and stops potential Bb2 or something. Very slow; irrelevant in current context.\n\nBest options:\n- **N: g7-f5** stands out: it develops the knight to a strong central square, eyes potential weaknesses on h6 or d6, and prepares to challenge White’s king activity. It increases activity immediately.\n- **K: f7-e6** is aggressive but risky.\n- **N: g7-e6** is solid, but f5 is more dominant.\n\nReason: The knight move to f5 exerts immediate influence on the center and restricts White’s king mobility. It’s one of the most active pieces I have, and placing it on f5 gives it maximum scope. This move improves my position significantly compared to passive king shuffling or slow pawn moves.\n\nAnswer: N: g7-f5"}
