12_23_16_30_08	Debug: {'system_prompt': '\n        You are a Chess player. Chess is a 2-player game playing on a 8x8 board, and the goal is to checkmate your opponent\'s king.\n        ## Pieces & Movement:\n\n        * King (K): Moves one square in any direction.\n\n        * Queen (Q): Moves any distance in any straight or diagonal line.\n\n        * Rook (R): Moves any distance horizontally or vertically.\n\n        * Bishop (B): Moves any distance diagonally.\n\n        * Knight (N): Moves in "L" shape: 2 squares one way + 1 square perpendicular.\n\n        * Pawn (P):\n\n            * Forward 1 square (never backward)\n\n            * First move: optionally 2 squares forward\n\n            * Captures diagonally forward\n\n            * Promotion: Reaches last rank → any piece (except king)\n        \n        ## Special rules: \n        * Castling: King moves 2 squares toward rook, rook jumps over. Conditions:\n\n            * Neither piece has moved\n\n            * No pieces between\n\n            * King not in check\n\n            * King doesn\'t pass through attacked squares\n\n        * En Passant: Pawn that moved 2 squares can be captured by adjacent enemy pawn as if it moved 1 square.\n\n        * Check: King under attack must escape immediately.\n\n        * Checkmate: King in check with no legal moves → game ends.\n\n        * Stalemate: King not in check but has no legal moves → draw.\n\n        ## Board and Chess piece Representation:\n        * Board is represented as [a-h][1-8]\n        * Origin: bottom-left corner (a1) \n        * White side: Bottom (ranks 1-2)\n        * Black side: Top (ranks 7-8)\n        * X-axis: Files `a` to `h` (left to right)\n        * Y-axis: Ranks `1` to `8` (bottom to top)\n\n        ## Piece encoding format: `[Type][Color][2Step][Moved]`\n        \n        * Type (Piece Type):\n            * `K` - King\n            * `Q`- Queen\n            * `R`- Rook\n            * `B` - Bishop\n            * `K` - Knight\n            * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly give a overview of the situation, evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Situation: [Your Observation]\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as Black.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |    |    |    |    |    |KBF1|    |PBF0| 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |PWF1| 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |PBF1|KWF1|    |    |    |NBF1|    |    | 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a5-a4, N: f5-d4, N: f5-d6, N: f5-e3, N: f5-e7, N: f5-g3, N: f5-g7, N: f5-h4, N: f5-h6, K: f7-e6, K: f7-e7, K: f7-e8, K: f7-f6, K: f7-f8, K: f7-g6, K: f7-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and my king is on f7, surrounded by limited friendly pieces. The only active piece I have is the knight on f5 and a pawn on a5. The white pawn on g6 is one square away from promoting, which poses an immediate threat—if it reaches g7 or g8 unchecked, White could promote and likely win. My king is currently not in check, but its mobility is restricted. I must act quickly to prevent White’s pawn from queening.\n\nEvaluation: \n- P: a5-a4 – Pushing the pawn does nothing urgent; it doesn’t threaten anything nor stops White’s g6-pawn.\n- N: f5-d4 – Moves knight inward, possibly for central control, but doesn’t address the g6 pawn threat.\n- N: f5-d6 – Attacks g6! This would capture the advancing white pawn next move if it stays. Strong candidate.\n- N: f5-e3 / e7 / g3 / g7 / h4 / h6 – These moves either go to less active squares or don’t pressure g6.\n- K: f7-e6 / f6 / g6 – King moving toward center or g-file might help support action, but g6 is currently attacked by White’s pawn on g6? Wait—no, g6 has PWF1 (White pawn), so f7-g6 would be illegal (occupied). Actually, g6 has a **White pawn**, so Black king cannot go there.\n- K: f7-e7/e8/f8/g8 – King retreats or lateral moves, passive in this position.\n\nCrucially, **N: f5-d6** threatens the White pawn on g6 directly. If White plays passively, we can play ...Nd6xg6, stopping promotion. Other knight moves don’t apply such pressure.\n\nBut wait — is g6 actually occupied by a White pawn? Yes: PWF1 on g6. So Nd6 attacks that square. However, **f5-d6** lands on d6, which is empty and safe. This is a good preparatory move.\n\nEven better: **N: f5-g7+**? That’s in the list: N: f5-g7 — this jumps directly to g7, where the White pawn *just left*? No — the pawn is on g6 now, not g7. So g7 is empty. But jumping to g7 with the knight would **attack g6 directly**, and also fork potential future promotions. More importantly, **Nf5-g7+** checks the White king indirectly? Not unless the White king is on e8 or f8. But we don’t see White’s king.\n\nWait — there's a **KWF1** on b5? That’s a White king on b5? That seems extremely exposed. Is that possible?\n\nLooking back at board:\n\n> 5 |PBF1|KWF1|    |    |    |NBF1|    |    | 5\n\nSo yes — **White king is on b5**, which is deep in Black’s side and highly vulnerable.\n\nThat changes everything.\n\nNow, **N: f5-d6** is still good, but let’s look at **N: f5-e7** — goes to e7, not threatening much.\n\nBut **N: f5-d4** — controls c2, e2, etc., but not urgent.\n\nWait — **N: f5-e7**, **N: f5-g7** — both go near the center or kingside, but the real opportunity is **attacking the enemy king on b5**.\n\nCan the knight attack it? From f5 to... let’s see: distance to b5 is file diff 4, rank diff 0 — too far.\n\nBut what about **knight fork ideas**?\n\nAlternatively, consider **N: f5-d4** — from d4, it attacks c2, e2, b5? Knight on d4 attacks: b3, c6, e6, f3, f5, b5? Yes! **d4 attacks b5** — the square where the White king is!\n\nSo **N: f5-d4** puts a knight directly attacking the White king — **check**!\n\nIs that correct?\n\nKnight moves in L-shape: from f5 to d4 is (-2 files, -1 rank) → valid L-shape.\n\nFrom d4, knight attacks b5? b5 is -2 files, +1 rank from d4? d4 → c6 (+1r, -1f), b5 is (-2f, +1r) → yes: two left, one up → that’s a knight move. So yes, **Nf5-d4 gives check to the king on b5**.\n\nThis is critical.\n\nMoreover, since the White king is on b5 and under attack, it must respond: move, capture, or block. But the knight is on d4, protected? By whom? Only potentially by the king or other pieces, but currently no direct defender — but White may not be able to capture it easily.\n\nAre any White pieces attacking d4? Unlikely. Pawn on a5? No. Pawn on g6? Far away. King on b5 — can it move? Let’s see escape squares: a4, a6, b4, b6, c4, c6. Many of these may be under Black control.\n\nBut more importantly: **N: f5-d4+** is a discovered check? No — it’s a direct check by the knight.\n\nAnd White has no interposition — knight cannot be blocked by pawns usually, and likely no piece can interpose.\n\nSo White must move the king or capture the knight.\n\nCan White capture the knight on d4? Which pieces?\n\n- Pawn on a5? No.\n- Pawn on g6? No.\n- King? From b5 to d4 is two files and one rank — not a king move.\n- Any other White piece? We don’t see any.\n\nSo **only way to escape is for White king to move**.\n\nAvailable squares: a4, a6, b4, b6, c4, c6.\n\na4: is it attacked? By Black’s pawn on a5? Yes — pawn on a5 (PBF1) attacks b4, but not a4. a4 is diagonally forward? Black pawn on a5 moves down? No — Black pawns move **upward**, from rank 7 to 8? Wait no.\n\nStandard chess: Black moves **down** the board? No — ranks: Black starts on 8, White on 1.\n\nBut in standard orientation:\n- White pawns move **up** (increasing rank)\n- Black pawns move **down** (decreasing rank)\n\nSo Black pawn on a5 is on rank 5 — to promote, it goes to a1.\n\nThus, Black pawn on a5 moves down: a5 → a4 → a3 etc.\n\nIt captures diagonally down: so from a5, it captures on b4.\n\nTherefore, **b4 is attacked by Black’s pawn on a5**.\n\nSo if White king tries to go to b4, it would be captured.\n\nSimilarly, a4: not attacked by any Black piece visible.\n\na6: not attacked.\n\nb6: not obviously attacked.\n\nc4: within reach of knight? Knight on d4 attacks c6, e6, f5, f3, e2, c2 — not c4. c4 is adjacent — king can go there.\n\nBut crucially, **after Nf5-d4+, White king must flee**, losing time, and likely allowing ...Nd4xc2 or similar follow-up.\n\nMoreover, this knight check **wins material or leads to decisive attack**, given the king's exposure.\n\nCompare to other moves:\n- N: f5-d6 — solid, stops g6 pawn from advancing freely, but allows White to consolidate.\n- N: f5-g7 — attacks g6 pawn? From g7, knight attacks f5, h5, e6, e8, f8 — not g6. g6 is adjacent — no, knight on g7 attacks: e8, f5, h5, e6? Two up one left: g7 → f5? That’s down two, left one — yes. But g6 is down one — not a knight move. So no.\n\nSo **N: f5-d4+ is a check**, strong tactical shot.\n\nIs it in the move list? Yes: **N: f5-d4** is listed.\n\nNo promotion needed — it’s a knight.\n\nTherefore, this is a powerful forcing move.\n\nReason: N: f5-d4 delivers check to the exposed White king on b5, forcing it to move immediately and likely lose material or tempo, while increasing Black’s initiative. It capitalizes on the rare vulnerability of the enemy king deep in our territory. Alternative moves like N-d6 are positional but fail to exploit this tactical opportunity.\n\nAnswer: N: f5-d4"}
