\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[K: f5-e4, K: f5-e5, K: f5-f4, K: f5-f6, K: f5-g4, K: f5-g5, K: f5-g6, N: g3-e2, N: g3-e4, N: g3-f1, N: g3-h1, P: h5-h4]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is nearly empty. My king is on f5 (KBF1), and I have a knight on g3 (NBF1) and a pawn on h5 (PBF1). The only white piece visible is a king on d7 (KWF1). The game is in an endgame phase, with minimal material left. My goal is to checkmate White or make progress toward a winning position.\n\nThe Black king on f5 is central and active, while the White king on d7 is somewhat passive but not immediately under threat. My knight on g3 has potential mobility, and the pawn on h5 could potentially advance to promote if supported. However, White’s king is far from the h-file, so immediate promotion might be difficult. The key is to coordinate my pieces to restrict the White king and aim for a mating net or force zugzwang.\n\nEvaluation:\n- King moves (f5-e4, e5, f4, f6, g4, g5, g6): Moving the king closer to the center or up the board may help control key squares. f5-e5 centralizes the king further. f5-g6 or f5-g5 heads toward the h-pawn’s promotion path. f5-f6 advances toward the 6th rank, supporting the knight or preparing to escort the pawn.\n- Knight moves (g3-e2, e4, f1, h1): These mostly retreat or go sideways. e4 is a strong central square, possibly eyeing f2 or g3 later. But e2, f1, h1 seem regressive and lose activity.\n- N: g3-e4 stands out — it places the knight centrally, controls key light squares, and may support future operations involving d6, f6, or g5.\n- P: h5-h4 advances the pawn one step toward promotion. This is a small but meaningful push — it gains space and keeps the option open to play h4-h3 later, threatening h3-h2. However, without king support, it may become weak.\n- Best progress comes from improving piece activity and king coordination.\n\nReason: While advancing the pawn (h5-h4) seems thematic in a king+pawn endgame, my king is currently distant. Instead, maximizing piece activity now is better. The knight move N: g3-e4 improves the knight to a more active central post, where it can influence both kingside and center, and supports potential maneuvers like ...Kf5-g5 or ...Kf5-f6. It doesn’t lose tempo and keeps all options open. In contrast, pushing the pawn now without king support may allow White to eventually capture it or gain counterplay.\n\nAnswer: N: g3-e4'}
