ush.\n- K: f5-g6 would aim to escort the pawn, but currently f5 is well-placed.\n- However, **N: e4-g5** places the knight aggressively, eyes potential entry points, and keeps options open.\n\nBut more importantly: **K: f5-g5** is not listed, so I cannot follow up immediately. Instead, **N: e4-g5** controls key territory and threatens to jump to f7 or h7, disrupting any White king activity.\n\nWait — actually, **K: f5-g5** *is* a valid move (listed as K: f5-g5). That’s critical.\n\nRe-evaluating:\n- K: f5-g5 advances the king closer to the h-pawn, supporting its advance.\n- After Kf5-g5, next move h5-h4 is followed by Kh5, etc., promoting the pawn.\n- But if I play K: f5-g5 now, White can respond Kd4-d5 or similar, but given that it\'s Black\'s turn, we want to maximize progress.\n\nBest plan: Support the h-pawn’s promotion. It\'s on h5, and with king on f5, we can march it down.\n\nOptimal path:\n1. Kf5-g5 — get closer to support h5-h4-h3-h2-h1=Q\n2. Then h5-h4, then king goes to g4, etc.\n\nBut wait — **K: f5-g5** is available.\n\nAlso, **N: e4-g5** blocks the king from going to g5? No — both can coexist.\n\nBut note: if I move the knight to g5, it occupies g5, blocking my own king.\n\nSo **N: e4-g5** is bad because it blocks the ideal king path.\n\nBetter: **K: f5-g5** — king advances to g5, supporting h5-h4 and preparing to escort the pawn.\n\nThen next move h5-h4, and so on.\n\nIs there a faster win?\n\nWhat about **K: f5-f6**? That heads toward the center, but away from the pawn.\n\nNo — g5 is better.\n\n**K: f5-g6**? Too far; g6 is adjacent to h5, but g5 is better for supporting h4.\n\nActually, **K: f5-g5** is excellent — it attacks h5? No, h5 is occupied by my own pawn.\n\nBut g5 is the ideal square to support h4 and then h3.\n\nYes.\n\nAlternatively, **P: h5-h4** — push the pawn first.\n\nBut then White king might come closer.\n\nBut with king on f5, after h5-h4, White king is still far.\n\nHowever, pushing the pawn now loses a tempo — better to centralize