ks.\n\nBut White has no rook — so no checks to fear.\n\nBut still, g6 is a more advanced position.\n\nSo **K: f5-g6** is excellent.\n\nBut what about **N: e4-g5**? That places the knight on g5, which is a strong outpost, and supports a future f7 or h7 push, but not directly helpful for the pawn.\n\nThe knight is better used elsewhere? But no targets.\n\nAlternatively, **N: e4-c5** or others — but those head to the wrong side.\n\nAll knight moves except g3, g5 are heading away from the action.\n\ng3 and g5 are plausible.\n\nBut **N: e4-g5** controls f7, h7, and f3, but doesn\'t help the h-pawn directly.\n\nWhereas king moves do.\n\nSo king move is better.\n\nNow between:\n- **K: f5-g6**\n- **K: f5-g5**\n- **P: h5-h4**\n\n**K: f5-g6** is the most aggressive and supportive.\n\nAfter Kf5-g6, White king is still far, and Black can push h5-h4 next, then knight can assist if needed.\n\nBut let\'s see if **P: h5-h4** first is better.\n\nIf I play **P: h5-h4**, then White plays Kd4-d5, aiming to cut across.\n\nBut Black can reply Kf5-g5, then next h4-h3, etc.\n\nBut after **K: f5-g6**, if I play that, then h5-h4 is even stronger because king is already nearby.\n\nSo **K: f5-g6** gains a tempo.\n\nMoreover, **K: f5-g6** is a quiet but powerful move.\n\nOther king moves like e5, e6 are toward the center — irrelevant.\n\nf4, f6 — less useful.\n\ng4 — not available.\n\nSo top candidates: **K: f5-g6**, **K: f5-g5**, **P: h5-h4**\n\nBest: **K: f5-g6** — places king optimally.\n\nBut wait — is g6 safe? No enemy pieces, so yes.\n\nAnd it\'s not blocked.\n\nSo yes.\n\nAlternatively, **N: e4-g3** — supports f5, but not useful.\n\nOr **N: e4-f6+** — check? Is that possible?\n\nN: e4-f6 — is f6 attacked?\n\nWhite king is on d4 — no piece on f6.\n\nBut N: e4-f6 is a valid move (listed).\n\nIf I play N: e4-f6, it checks the White king? Let\'s see:\n\nKnight on e4 to f6 — is d4 under attack?\n\nKnight on f6 attacks: e8, g8, h7, h5, g4, e4, d5, etc.\n\nFrom f6, knight attacks: d5, e4, g4, h5, h7, g8, e8, d7.\n\nd4 is not attacked — d4 is two files and two ranks away? Knight moves 2 in one, 1 in other.\n\nFrom f6 to d4: file difference 2 (f to d), rank difference 2 (6 to 4) — that’s a diagonal, not L-shape. So no, d4 is not attacked.\n\nSo N: e4-f6 does not check.\n\nIt just occupies f6.\n\nBut f6 is closer to the kingside.\n\nStill, not as useful as king movement.\n\nThus, best move is **K: f5-g6** — centralizes the king near the pawn, prepares to escort it to promotion.\n\nReason: Advancing the king to g6 optimally supports the h5 pawn\'s journey to promotion, placing the king in front of the pawn on the adjacent file, which is crucial in rook pawn endgames. This move increases winning chances by minimizing White\'s counterplay and maximizing king activity.\n\nAnswer: K: f5-g6'}
