\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[N: e4-c3, N: e4-c5, N: e4-d2, N: e4-d6, N: e4-f2, N: e4-f6, N: e4-g3, N: e4-g5, K: g6-f5, K: g6-f6, K: g6-f7, K: g6-g5, K: g6-g7, K: g6-h6, K: g6-h7, P: h5-h4]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is nearly empty. My king is on g6 (KBF1), and I have a knight on e4 (NBF1) and a pawn on h5 (PBF1). The only white piece visible is a king on c7 (KWF1), which is deep in my territory. White's king is extremely exposed, far from its own side, while my king is central to the board. The game is in an endgame phase, and the key objective is to use my pieces to restrict the white king and aim for checkmate.\n\nMy knight on e4 is well-placed and has multiple outposts. My king on g6 is close to the center and can support both defense and attack. The pawn on h5 is one step away from promotion if advanced, but currently it's not supported and could be vulnerable if pushed without purpose. However, advancing it to h4 prepares potential push to h3 or supports mating patterns later.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Knight moves:\n  - N: e4-c3 / e4-c5 / e4-d2 / e4-f2: These move the knight toward the queenside or back ranks, far from the action. They don’t threaten the white king meaningfully.\n  - N: e4-d6: Attacks c8 and e8 squares; could help restrict the white king if it retreats, but white king is on c7 — this might cut off escape, but it's still distant.\n  - N: e4-f6: This is strong — f6 is adjacent to g7 and e7, supporting a potential mate net around the white king. It also defends g7 if needed and pressures the center.\n  - N: e4-g3 / e4-g5: These go toward the kingside, possibly supporting the h-pawn, but are less effective immediately against Kc7.\n\n- King moves:\n  - K: g6-f7 / g6-f6 / g6-f5 / g6-g5 / g6-h6 / g6-h7: All bring my king closer to the center or kingside. K: g6-f7 is particularly strong — it centralizes the king and directly threatens the white king on c7? No — wait, c7 is too far. Actually, the black king moving toward d-file would be better, but these moves are slow.\n\nWait — critical observation: White king is on **c7**, which is very deep in black’s side. But with no other white pieces, and no immediate threat to my king, I should focus on **activating my pieces to trap the white king**.\n\nBut the white king is on c7, and my knight on e4 can control key squares. Let's see:\n\n- If I play N: e4-d6, then the knight attacks b7, c8, e8, f7. The white king on c7 will have limited mobility — currently, it can go to b7, b8, c8, d8, d7 — but if I control b8 and d8, I can box it.\n\nBut my pieces are too far. Alternatively, **my best plan is to promote the h-pawn**. It's on h5, one step from h4, then h3, h2, h1=Q. That could be decisive.\n\nCurrently, P: h5-h4 is a valid move. It advances the pawn, and since there's no white piece near, it's safe. After h4, next move h3, then h2, then promote. White king is on c7 — it would take at least 5-6 moves to reach the kingside, by which time I could promote and deliver checkmate.\n\nMoreover, my king can assist if needed, but even without assistance, a queen on h1 would dominate.\n\nTherefore, the most forcing and winning move is to **advance the pawn**.\n\nOther knight moves do not lead to immediate progress. The knight is already active, but the pawn promotion is a concrete win.\n\nReason: Promoting the h-pawn is a direct path to a decisive material advantage. With a new queen, I can easily checkmate the lone white king, even if it's active. Delaying the pawn advance risks allowing the white king to approach, but h4-h3-h2-h1=Q happens in just three moves, and the white king is too far (minimum 5–6 moves from c7 to h-file) to stop it. Thus, starting the advance now is optimal.\n\nAnswer: P: h5-h4"}
