\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[N: e4-c3, N: e4-c5, N: e4-d2, N: e4-d6, N: e4-f2, N: e4-f6, N: e4-g3, N: e4-g5, K: g6-f5, K: g6-f6, K: g6-f7, K: g6-g5, K: g6-g7, K: g6-h5, K: g6-h6, Q: h7-g7, Q: h7-f7, Q: h7-e7, Q: h7-d7, Q: h7-c7, Q: h7-b7, Q: h7-a7, Q: h7-g8, Q: h7-h6, Q: h7-h5, Q: h7-h4, Q: h7-h3, Q: h7-h2, Q: h7-h1, Q: h7-h8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is nearly empty. The key pieces are my king on g6 (KBF1), my knight on e4 (NBF1), and my queen on h7 (QBF1). White’s king is on f6 (KWF1), extremely close to mine—only one square separates the kings. This proximity creates significant tactical danger, especially since my king is exposed and White could potentially force a check or even deliver checkmate if I’m not careful. However, I have strong offensive potential with my queen and knight.\n\nMy king is in potential danger from the White king, which can advance and restrict my mobility. My knight has several possible moves, some attacking key central squares, while my queen has long-range threats along the 7th rank and down the h-file.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Knight moves:\n  - N: e4-c3, c5, d2, d6, f2, f6, g3, g5 — most of these don’t immediately contribute to attack or safety. \n  - N: e4-f6+ would fork or check the White king on f6? But f6 is occupied by KWF1 — cannot move there.\n  - All knight moves are non-checking and don’t threaten immediate material gain.\n- King moves:\n  - K: g6-f5, g6-g5, g6-h5, etc., bring my king closer to the center or edge but risk walking into checks or being trapped.\n  - K: g6-f7 moves toward the center but approaches the enemy king.\n  - K: g6-g7 or g6-h6 retreats slightly, possibly safer.\n- Queen moves:\n  - Q: h7-g7, f7, e7, ..., a7 — horizontal threats along 7th rank.\n  - Q: h7-h1 through h8 — vertical control of h-file.\n  - Most importantly: **Q: h7-h8** delivers **checkmate**, because:\n    - After Qh7 → h8, the White king on f6 is under attack from the queen on h8.\n    - The king cannot capture the queen (no piece can).\n    - The king cannot run: f6 → f7 is attacked by queen on h8; f6 → g5, g6, f5, e5, e6, e7 are all either off the board or not available.\n    - Let's verify:\n      - King on f6. Attacked squares by Qh8: entire h-file and 8th rank. So f6 is not directly attacked unless the queen controls f6 diagonally.\n      - Wait: Qh8 attacks along the 8th rank (so g8, f8, e8...) and along the h-file (h7, h6...). But **does it attack f6?**\n        - From h8 to f6: that’s two left, two down — diagonal? Yes! h8 → g7 → f6. That’s a diagonal of three squares: h8–g7–f6. So yes, **the queen on h8 attacks f6 via the diagonal a1–h8**.\n\nWait: h8 to f6 is two files left and two ranks down — that is indeed a diagonal move. So yes, **queen on h8 attacks f6**.\n\nBut is the path clear?\n- From h8 to f6: passes through g7. Is g7 occupied? No — currently empty. So yes, the diagonal is open.\n\nTherefore: **Q: h7-h8 puts the White king on f6 in check**.\n\nNow, can the White king escape?\nAvailable moves from f6:\n- f6 → f5: is f5 attacked by Qh8? f5 is one down from f6 — not on same diagonal/rank/file as h8. Distance: h8 to f5 = 2 left, 3 down — not aligned. So f5 is safe? Wait — no: queen on h8 controls:\n  - Entire h-file: h1–h8\n  - Entire 8th rank: a8–h8\n  - Diagonals: a1–h8 and b1–h7, etc.\n\nThe a1–h8 diagonal includes: a1, b2, c3, d4, e5, f6, g7, h8.\n\nYes! f6 is on the a1–h8 diagonal. So queen on h8 attacks f6.\n\nNow, where can the White king go?\n\nFrom f6:\n- f5: is f5 on the a1–h8 diagonal? f5 = file f, rank 5 → f ≠ 5, so no. Not on that diagonal. Not on h-file or 8th rank. So **f5 is not attacked by Qh8** → escape square?\n- But wait: is there any other attacker? Only Black pieces are queen, knight, king. None else.\nSo if White king goes to f5, it escapes the check.\n\nWait — then **Q: h7-h8 does NOT checkmate — only checks**.\n\nBut is it even legal?\n\nAfter Qh7 → h8, the White king is on f6, attacked by queen on h8 via diagonal.\n\nCan White get out of check?\n\nKing moves from f6:\n- f5: not attacked → valid\n- g5: g5 — is it attacked? g5 vs h8: difference 1 left, 3 down → not aligned → not attacked\n- g6: g6 — h8 to g6: 1 left, 2 down → not diagonal (would require equal steps) → not attacked\n- e5, e6, f7, g7 — g7 is attacked (on diagonal), so g7 is not safe\n- But f7: f7 — h8 to f7: 2 left, 1 down → not diagonal → not attacked\n\nSo multiple escape squares: f5, g5, e5, e6, f7, etc.\n\nSo **Q: h7-h8 gives check, but not checkmate**.\n\nHowever, is it safe for me?\n\nAfter Qh7 → h8, is my queen protected?\n\nQueen on h8 — can White king take it? From f6 to h8: 2 right, 2 up → g7, h8 — yes, that’s a king move! f6 → g7 → h8. So f6 to h8 is two squares away — king can only move one. So **cannot capture**.\n\nNo other White pieces. So queen is safe.\n\nBut after Qh7-h8+, White plays Kf6-f5 or similar, escaping.\n\nBut now my queen dominates the long diagonal and the h-file.\n\nStill, better options?\n\nWhat about **Q: h7-g7**? That attacks the king on f6 directly?\n\nFrom g7 to f6: adjacent — yes, diagonal. So queen on g7 attacks f6.\n\nIs that check?\n\nYes: Qh7 → g7, then queen on g7 attacks f6 diagonally (one square away).\n\nAnd can the White king capture? f6 → g7 — yes, king can move one square — so **White can capture the queen** with Kf6xg7.\n\nThen I lose my queen for nothing. Not good.\n\nSimilarly, Q: h7-f7 — f7 is adjacent to f6? Yes — same file. So Qf7 attacks Kf6 vertically.\n\nThen White plays Kf6xf7 — captures queen again.\n\nSame issue.\n\nAny queen move to a square adjacent to f6 allows capture.\n\nOnly safe checks are from a distance.\n\nSo **Q: h7-h8+** is a **safe check**, because h8 is two squares diagonally from f6 — king cannot reach it.\n\nOther checking moves:\n- Q: h7-e7? e7 to f6 — adjacent? e7 to f6 is one right, one down — diagonal — yes, adjacent. So Kf6xe7 — captures.\n- Similarly, d7, c7, etc., are all within capture range if adjacent.\n\nOnly non-adjacent check is **Q: h7-h8**, which checks from a distance.\n\nAlternatively, **Q: h7-d7**? d7 to f6: 2 right, 1 up — not aligned. Not a check.\n\nOnly queen moves that check are those on the diagonal a1–h8 (e.g., e5, f6, g7, h8) or same rank/file.\n\nOn same rank: 7th rank — h7 to a7: none attack f6 unless on f7.\n\nOn same file: h-file — h8 is only one above.\n\nOn diagonal a1–h8: squares like e5, f6, g7, h8.\n\nSo only queen moves that give check are:\n- h7 → g7: check, but capturable\n- h7 → f7: not on diagonal, but f7 is adjacent to f6 — yes, vertical check — but again, capturable\n- h7 → h8: check, not capturable (king too far)\n\nSo **Q: h7-h8 is the only safe check**.\n\nDelivering check forces the opponent to respond, gains tempo, and improves my position.\n\nAlternatively, could I play **N: e4-f6+**? But f6 is occupied by the White king — cannot move there.\n\nAll knight moves are non-checking.\n\nKing moves: K: g6-g7? Then I put my king on g7 — which is on the a1–h8 diagonal — and after that, White plays Qh8 (but I don’t have a queen yet) — no, White doesn’t have queen.\n\nBut if I play Kg6-g7, then White can play Kf6-f7? f7 is empty — yes, then White invades.\n\nOr worse, if I play any king move, White might play Kf6-f7 or f5, gaining space.\n\nBest idea: **deliver check with queen to h8**, forcing the White king to move, while I gain initiative.\n\nEven though it doesn’t win immediately, it seizes the initiative.\n\nNo other move threatens anything.\n\nSo **Q: h7-h8** is best.\n\nReason: It delivers a safe check (queen cannot be captured), forces the White king to move, and activates my most powerful piece to a dominant diagonal. All other queen moves either don’t check or allow recapture. Knight and king moves do not create immediate threats.\n\nAnswer: Q: h7-h8"}
