any other square, I lose the opposition.\n\nTherefore, the goal is to minimize White’s progress.\n\nBest practice in king and pawn endgames (even though no pawns) is to **centralize** the king.\n\nThus, among the options, **K: c4-d4** is best — it centralizes the king and keeps flexibility.\n\nAlternatively, **K: c4-b4** is also good, preparing to mirror.\n\nBut **K: c4-d4** gives the most scope.\n\nAfter Kd4, if White plays Kd5, I play Kd3, taking the opposition.\n\nIf White plays Kb5, I play Kb4, meeting it head-on.\n\nSo **K: c4-d4** is the most flexible and principled move.\n\nReason: Centralizing the king maximizes its mobility and prepares to oppose the white king effectively, maintaining drawing chances (which is the best possible outcome in a king-only endgame).\n\nAnswer: K: c4-d4'}
