 ends in a draw immediately.\n\nBut since the White king is far away (b7), none of the current moves lead directly to stalemate. All moves are safe in that regard.\n\nBest practical choice: Centralize the king to maintain maximum mobility and prepare for potential opposition play (in case more pieces were present — though they aren’t).\n\nf5 is central and offers good king activity. But wait — let's double-check distances.\n\nActually, all moves are roughly equal in value given the lack of material. However, moving **K: e5-f6** brings the black king up toward the top, increasing space and avoiding the center-bottom where passive placement could risk future inactivity.\n\nWait — better idea: **K: e5-e6** advances straight up the board, central file, maintaining symmetry and control of key central dark squares. This keeps maximum flexibility.\n\nAlternatively, **K: e5-f5** heads toward the center (d4, e5, f5, etc.), which is ideal for king activity.\n\nAll are equally non-winning, but **K: e5-f5** goes to a central square, maximizing future move options.\n\nReason: Centralizing the king maximizes mobility and theoretical influence over the board. Although this position is drawn due to insufficient material, playing actively ensures that if White makes an illegal attempt (e.g., stepping into check), we retain optimal positioning. Among all available moves, f5 is one of the most active.\n\nAnswer: K: e5-f5"}
